{"id":"n-20261017-xjb219","category":"health","display_title":"Why Non-Smokers Are Becoming Addicted to Vaping","display_title_he":"מדוע אנשים שמעולם לא עישנו נלכדים בהתמכרות לסגריות אלקטרוניות","short_summary":"Recent data reveals that over one million British adults who never smoked tobacco now vape regularly or occasionally. Experts and former non-smokers reflect on the psychological patterns, heavy marketing, and cessation challenges driving this growing trend.","english_level":"C1","words":501,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ck62y33489zqo","title":"Why people who have never smoked are getting hooked on vaping","published_at":"2026-10-16T23:49:16.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-16T23:49:16.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-17T06:06:10.072Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["When e-cigarettes first arrived on the market, health authorities envisaged them primarily as a harm-reduction instrument designed to help lifelong cigarette smokers quit tobacco. However, recent data from the UK’s Office for National Statistics (ONS) reveals a surprising shift: more than one million British adults who have never smoked tobacco are now regular or occasional vapers. Across Britain, approximately 5.4 million people aged 16 and over currently use e-cigarettes, comfortably surpassing the 4.9 million adults who smoke traditional cigarettes—a demographic whose numbers continue to decline steadily.","For many non-smokers, what begins as casual experimentation rapidly metamorphoses into a deeply ingrained daily habit. According to GP Dr Helen Wall, many patients develop a subtle psychological dependence on vaping to help regulate their emotions. The physical routine itself—having a sleek device in hand and bringing it to the mouth—creates an subconscious sensory pattern. Gracey Mungulu, a 21-year-old Londoner who picked up the habit at school despite initial aversion, describes vaping as a mindless ritual that accompanies everyday activities like scrolling on social media. For others, such as 33-year-old domestic abuse campaigner Malin Andersson, taking up vaping during a stressful relationship breakup provided a comforting dopamine hit, akin to an adult pacifier, before she successfully quit earlier this year.","Academic researchers offer nuanced interpretations of this statistical surge. Professor Sarah Jackson, from the University College London Alcohol and Tobacco Research Group, notes that while rising nicotine consumption among young adults raises valid concerns, vaping may have diverted some individuals away from traditional smoking, which carries far severe health risks. Nonetheless, a 2024 UCL study confirmed that overall nicotine usage among young adults in England has climbed even as smoking rates plummeted, implying that a substantial cohort of vapers would never have picked up a tobacco habit in the first place.","Prominent industry marketing, historically featuring vibrant colours and appealing sweet flavours like banana ice, has drawn criticism for fuelling the misconception that e-cigarettes are completely harmless and non-addictive. Hazel Cheeseman, from the charity Action on Smoking and Health, highlights that aggressive commercial promotion has actively enticed non-smokers. Although the National Health Service (NHS) emphasizes that vaping nicotine is significantly less damaging than smoking combusted tobacco, it warns non-smokers and under-18s against adopting the practice, emphasizing that the long-term health ramifications remain uncertain. In response to youth uptake, the UK banned disposable vapes last year and continues to deliberate on stricter marketing restrictions.","Giving up e-cigarettes presents formidable challenges due to nicotine's highly addictive nature, which triggers physical withdrawal symptoms such as anxiety and irritability. While the NHS recommends gradually reducing nicotine concentrations or utilizing replacement therapies, professional support remains inconsistent; a recent survey revealed that fewer than half of healthcare providers in England offer dedicated vaping cessation services. Alarmingly, attempting to quit can sometimes backfire. Alex, a 26-year-old from Manchester, managed to stop vaping while travelling abroad, but lacking access to e-cigarettes, she unexpectedly started smoking cigarettes instead—a counterproductive outcome that highlights the complex hurdles facing a generation caught in nicotine's changing grasp."],"facts":[{"fact":"According to Office for National Statistics (ONS) survey data, more than 1 million adults in the UK who have never smoked tobacco now use e-cigarettes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Approximately 5.4 million people aged 16 and over in Britain vape, while about 4.9 million adults smoke cigarettes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Nearly one in six people aged 25 to 34 in the UK use e-cigarettes daily or occasionally.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Disposable vapes were banned in the UK last year, and the government is examining vape marketing, including bright colours and sweet flavours.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A 2024 UCL study found that nicotine use had increased among young adults in England even though cigarette smoking had declined.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A survey showed that fewer than half (45%) of healthcare providers in England offer dedicated vaping cessation support.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"e-cigarettes","lemma":"e-cigarette","pos":"noun","he":"סיגריות אלקטרוניות","name":false},{"text":"arrived","lemma":"arrive","pos":"verb","he":"הגיעו","name":false},{"text":"market","lemma":"market","pos":"noun","he":"שוק","name":false},{"text":"health authorities","lemma":"health authority","pos":"compound","he":"רשויות הבריאות","name":false},{"text":"envisaged","lemma":"envisage","pos":"verb","he":"חזו, ראו בעיני רוחם","name":false},{"text":"p